\id JHN Transparent Bible (working title) - pilot draft
\h John
\toc1 The Gospel According to John
\toc2 John
\toc3 John
\mt1 John
\c 1
\s1 The Word
\p
\v 1 In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Gk.: \fqa logos\ft , “word”, also “message” or “reason”.\f*
\v 2 He was in the beginning with God.
\v 3 All things were made through him, and without him nothing was made that was made.\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Or: \fqa …and without him nothing was made. What was made in him was life \ft (punctuating with v. 4). The oldest Greek manuscripts have no punctuation.\f*
\v 4 In him was life, and the life was the light of men.
\v 5 And the light shines in the darkness, and the darkness did not grasp it.\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Or: \fqa overcome it\ft ; or \fqa comprehend it \ft (KJV). The Greek verb \fqa katalambanō \ft can mean both “seize” and “understand”.\f*
\p
\v 6 There was a man sent from God, whose name was John.
\v 7 He came as a witness, to bear witness about the light, that all might believe through him.
\v 8 He was not the light, but \add came\add* to bear witness about the light.
\p
\v 9 That was the true light, which gives light to every man coming into the world.\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Or: \fqa The true light, which gives light to every man, was coming into the world\ft . The Greek allows both.\f*
\v 10 He was in the world, and the world was made through him, yet the world did not know him.
\v 11 He came to his own, and his own people did not receive him.\f + \fr 1:11 \ft Gk.: \fqa ta idia \ft … \fqa hoi idioi\ft , “his own things” … “his own people”.\f*
\v 12 But to all who received him, who believed in his name, he gave the right to become children of God,\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Gk.: \fqa exousia\ft ; KJV \fqa power\ft .\f*
\v 13 who were born, not of blood, nor of the will of the flesh, nor of the will of man, but of God.\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Lit.: \fqa bloods\f*
\p
\v 14 And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us, and we beheld his glory, glory as of the only begotten from the Father, full of grace and truth.\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Lit.: \fqa pitched his tent\ft , \fqa tabernacled\ft .\f*\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Or: \fqa the one and only\ft , \fqa the unique one\ft . Gk. \fqa monogenēs\ft ; also in v. 18 and 3:16, 18.\f*
\v 15 John bore witness about him and cried out, saying, “This was he of whom I said, ‘He who comes after me ranks before me, because he was before me.’ ”
\v 16 And from his fullness we have all received, grace upon grace.\f + \fr 1:16 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa And\ft ; CT \fqa For\ft .\f*\f + \fr 1:16 \ft Lit.: \fqa grace for grace\f*
\v 17 For the law was given through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ.
\v 18 No one has ever seen God; the only begotten Son, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has made \add him\add* known.\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa the only begotten Son\ft ; CT \fqa the only begotten God\ft , or \fqa God, the only Son\ft .\f*
\s1 The Testimony of John
\p
\v 19 And this is the testimony of John, when the Jews sent priests and Levites from Jerusalem to ask him, “Who are you?”\f + \fr 1:19 \ft Note: In this Gospel “the Jews” often refers to the religious authorities in Jerusalem.\f*
\v 20 He confessed and did not deny, but confessed, “I am not the Christ.”\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Gk.: \fqa Christos\ft , “Anointed One”, the Greek for Messiah.\f*
\v 21 And they asked him, “What then? Are you Elijah?” He said, “I am not.” “Are you the Prophet?” And he answered, “No.”\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Gk.: \fqa Elias \ft (KJV), the Greek form of Elijah.\f*
\v 22 So they said to him, “Who are you? Let us give an answer to those who sent us. What do you say about yourself?”
\v 23 He said, “I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness, ‘\zpl Make straight\zpl* the way of the \nd LORD\nd*,’ as Isaiah the prophet said.”\f + \fr 1:23 \ft OT: Isaiah 40:3. \+nd LORD\+nd*: the Greek \fqa Kyrios \ft here stands for the divine name in the Hebrew.\f*
\p
\v 24 Now those who were sent were from the Pharisees.
\v 25 And they asked him and said to him, “Why then do you baptize, if you are not the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the Prophet?”
\v 26 John answered them, saying, “I baptize with water, but among \zpl you\zpl* stands one whom \zpl you\zpl* do not know.
\v 27 He it is who, coming after me, is preferred before me, the strap of whose sandal I am not worthy to untie.”\f + \fr 1:27 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa He it is who … is preferred before me\ft ; CT \fqa He who comes after me\ft .\f*
\v 28 These things took place in Bethabara beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing.\f + \fr 1:28 \ft Text: TR \fqa Bethabara\ft ; Maj., CT \fqa Bethany\ft .\f*
\p
\v 29 The next day John saw Jesus coming toward him, and said, “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!
\v 30 This is he of whom I said, ‘After me comes a man who ranks before me, because he was before me.’
\v 31 I myself did not know him, but so that he might be revealed to Israel, for this reason I came baptizing with water.”
\v 32 And John bore witness, saying, “I saw the Spirit descending from heaven like a dove, and it remained on him.
\v 33 I myself did not know him, but he who sent me to baptize with water said to me, ‘He on whom you see the Spirit descend and remain, this is he who baptizes with the Holy Spirit.’\f + \fr 1:33 \ft Note: KJV \fqa Holy Ghost\ft .\f*
\v 34 And I have seen and have borne witness that this is the Son of God.”
\s1 The First Disciples
\p
\v 35 The next day again John was standing with two of his disciples,
\v 36 and looking at Jesus as he walked, he said, “Behold, the Lamb of God!”
\v 37 The two disciples heard him speak, and they followed Jesus.
\v 38 Then Jesus turned, and seeing them following, said to them, “\wj What are \+zpl you\+zpl* seeking?\wj*” They said to him, “Rabbi” (which means, when translated, Teacher), “where are you staying?”
\v 39 He said to them, “\wj \+zpl Come\+zpl* and \+zpl see\+zpl*.\wj*” They came and saw where he was staying, and they stayed with him that day, for it was about the tenth hour.\f + \fr 1:39 \ft Text: TR \fqa see\ft ; Maj., CT \fqa you will see\ft .\f*\f + \fr 1:39 \ft Note: About four in the afternoon, counting from sunrise.\f*
\v 40 One of the two who heard John speak and followed him was Andrew, the brother of Simon Peter.
\v 41 He first found his own brother Simon and said to him, “We have found the Messiah” (which means, when translated, the Christ).\f + \fr 1:41 \ft Text: TR \fqa prōtos\ft , “he was the first” (before the other disciple); Maj., CT \fqa prōton\ft , “first of all”.\f*
\v 42 And he brought him to Jesus. And when Jesus looked at him, he said, “\wj You are Simon the son of Jonah. You shall be called Cephas\wj*” (which means, when translated, Peter).\f + \fr 1:42 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa Jonah \ft (KJV \fqa Jona\ft ); CT \fqa John\ft .\f*\f + \fr 1:42 \ft Note: \fqa Cephas \ft (Aramaic) and \fqa Peter \ft (Greek) both mean “rock” or “stone”.\f*
\p
\v 43 The next day Jesus wanted to go into Galilee, and he found Philip and said to him, “\wj Follow me.\wj*”
\v 44 Now Philip was from Bethsaida, the city of Andrew and Peter.
\v 45 Philip found Nathanael and said to him, “We have found him of whom Moses in the law, and also the prophets, wrote: Jesus of Nazareth, the son of Joseph.”
\v 46 Nathanael said to him, “Can anything good come out of Nazareth?” Philip said to him, “Come and see.”
\v 47 Jesus saw Nathanael coming toward him and said of him, “\wj Behold, an Israelite indeed, in whom is no deceit!\wj*”
\v 48 Nathanael said to him, “How do you know me?” Jesus answered and said to him, “\wj Before Philip called you, when you were under the fig tree, I saw you.\wj*”
\v 49 Nathanael answered and said to him, “Rabbi, you are the Son of God! You are the King of Israel!”
\v 50 Jesus answered and said to him, “\wj Because I said to you, ‘I saw you under the fig tree,’ do you believe? You will see greater things than these.\wj*”
\v 51 And he said to him, “\wj Truly, truly, I say to \+zpl you\+zpl*, hereafter \+zpl you\+zpl* will see heaven opened, and the angels of God ascending and descending on the Son of Man.\wj*”\f + \fr 1:51 \ft Gk.: \fqa amēn, amēn\ft ; KJV \fqa Verily, verily\ft .\f*\f + \fr 1:51 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa hereafter\ft ; CT omits.\f*\f + \fr 1:51 \ft OT: Genesis 28:12.\f*
\c 3
\s1 Jesus and Nicodemus
\p
\v 1 Now there was a man of the Pharisees named Nicodemus, a ruler of the Jews.
\v 2 This man came to Jesus by night and said to him, “Rabbi, we know that you are a teacher come from God, for no one can do these signs that you do unless God is with him.”\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Gk.: \fqa sēmeia\ft , “signs”; KJV \fqa miracles\ft .\f*
\v 3 Jesus answered and said to him, “\wj Truly, truly, I say to you, unless one is born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God.\wj*”\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Or: \fqa from above\ft . The Greek \fqa anōthen \ft means both. Nicodemus hears “again” (v. 4); in v. 31 the same word means “from above”. Also in v. 7.\f*
\v 4 Nicodemus said to him, “How can a man be born when he is old? Can he enter a second time into his mother’s womb and be born?”
\v 5 Jesus answered, “\wj Truly, truly, I say to you, unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God.\wj*
\v 6 \wj That which is born of the flesh is flesh, and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit.\wj*
\v 7 \wj Do not marvel that I said to you, ‘\+zpl You\+zpl* must be born again.’\wj*
\v 8 \wj The wind blows where it wishes, and you hear its sound, but you do not know where it comes from or where it goes. So it is with everyone who is born of the Spirit.\wj*”\f + \fr 3:8 \ft Gk.: \fqa pneuma\ft , meaning both “wind” and “Spirit”; Jesus plays on both senses.\f*
\p
\v 9 Nicodemus answered and said to him, “How can these things be?”
\v 10 Jesus answered and said to him, “\wj Are you the teacher of Israel, and do not know these things?\wj*
\v 11 \wj Truly, truly, I say to you, we speak what we know and bear witness to what we have seen, and \+zpl you\+zpl* do not receive our testimony.\wj*
\v 12 \wj If I have told \+zpl you\+zpl* earthly things and \+zpl you\+zpl* do not believe, how will \+zpl you\+zpl* believe if I tell \+zpl you\+zpl* heavenly things?\wj*
\v 13 \wj No one has ascended into heaven except he who came down from heaven, the Son of Man who is in heaven.\wj*\f + \fr 3:13 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa who is in heaven\ft ; CT omits.\f*
\v 14 \wj And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, so must the Son of Man be lifted up,\wj*\f + \fr 3:14 \ft OT: Numbers 21:8–9.\f*
\v 15 \wj that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life.\wj*\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa believes in him should not perish but\ft ; CT \fqa believes may have eternal life in him\ft .\f*
\p
\v 16 \wj For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life.\wj*\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Or: \fqa For in this way God loved the world\ft . Gk. \fqa houtōs \ft means “so” or “thus”.\f*\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Note: The Greek has no quotation marks. Jesus’ words may end at v. 15; vv. 16–21 may be the Gospel writer’s comment.\f*
\v 17 \wj For God did not send his Son into the world to judge the world, but that the world might be saved through him.\wj*\f + \fr 3:17 \ft Or: \fqa condemn \ft (KJV). Gk. \fqa krinō\ft , “judge”; also in v. 18, and \fqa krisis\ft , “judgment”, in v. 19.\f*
\v 18 \wj Whoever believes in him is not judged, but whoever does not believe is judged already, because he has not believed in the name of the only begotten Son of God.\wj*
\v 19 \wj And this is the judgment: that the light has come into the world, and men loved the darkness rather than the light, because their deeds were evil.\wj*
\v 20 \wj For everyone who does evil hates the light and does not come to the light, lest his deeds should be exposed.\wj*
\v 21 \wj But whoever practices the truth comes to the light, so that it may be clearly seen that his deeds have been done in God.\wj*”\f + \fr 3:21 \ft Lit.: \fqa does the truth\f*
\s1 John’s Testimony About Jesus
\p
\v 22 After these things Jesus and his disciples went into the land of Judea, and he remained there with them and was baptizing.
\v 23 John also was baptizing at Aenon near Salim, because there was much water there; and they were coming and being baptized.
\v 24 For John had not yet been put in prison.
\p
\v 25 Then a dispute arose between some of John’s disciples and the Jews about purification.\f + \fr 3:25 \ft Text: TR \fqa the Jews\ft ; Maj., CT \fqa a Jew\ft .\f*
\v 26 And they came to John and said to him, “Rabbi, he who was with you beyond the Jordan, to whom you have borne witness—behold, he is baptizing, and all are coming to him.”
\v 27 John answered and said, “A man can receive nothing unless it has been given to him from heaven.
\v 28 \zpl You\zpl* yourselves bear me witness that I said, ‘I am not the Christ,’ but, ‘I have been sent before him.’
\v 29 He who has the bride is the bridegroom. But the friend of the bridegroom, who stands and hears him, rejoices greatly because of the bridegroom’s voice. Therefore this joy of mine is now complete.\f + \fr 3:29 \ft Lit.: \fqa rejoices with joy\f*
\v 30 He must increase, but I must decrease.”
\p
\v 31 He who comes from above is above all. He who is from the earth is of the earth, and speaks of the earth. He who comes from heaven is above all.\f + \fr 3:31 \ft Note: The Greek has no quotation marks. John the Baptist’s words may continue through v. 36.\f*
\v 32 And what he has seen and heard, that he bears witness to, and no one receives his testimony.
\v 33 Whoever has received his testimony has set his seal to this, that God is true.
\v 34 For he whom God has sent speaks the words of God, for God does not give the Spirit by measure.\f + \fr 3:34 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa God does not give\ft ; CT \fqa he does not give\ft .\f*
\v 35 The Father loves the Son and has given all things into his hand.
\v 36 Whoever believes in the Son has eternal life; but whoever does not obey the Son shall not see life, but the wrath of God remains on him.\f + \fr 3:36 \ft Or: \fqa does not believe \ft (KJV). Gk. \fqa apeitheō\ft , “disobey”, a different word from “believe” earlier in the verse.\f*
